course: dictionary
lesson: 7160
title: The perimeter: the way all the way round
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The perimeter is the length all the way around a shape. If you walk around the edge of a field and come back to the start, the distance you walked is its perimeter. The perimeter of a place is its outer edge or border, like the fence around a field.
explain: In maths, you find a perimeter by adding up all the sides. «A square has four equal sides, so one side is the perimeter divided by 4.» If you know the perimeter, you can work backwards to find a side.
explain: Stories and everyday life use this word too. «Guards walk around the perimeter of the castle» means they patrol the outer edge. A ribbon around a cake or a border on a picture is a perimeter as well. When you meet the word, read the words around it: they will tell you whether it means a distance, an edge, or both.
fact: Perimeter is a noun. One is a perimeter, and more than one are perimeters.
fact: The perimeter of a shape is the total length of all its sides added together.
fact: «The perimeter of a rectangle is twice the length plus twice the width.»
fact: The perimeter of a place means its outer edge or border, like a fence around a garden.
exercise: «A square has four equal sides, so one side is the perimeter divided by 4.» A square has a perimeter of 28 cm. How long is one side? | 5 cm | *7 cm | 24 cm | 32 cm
exercise: Guards walk around the perimeter of the castle. What is the perimeter here? | The rooms inside the castle | *The outer edge all the way around | The tallest tower | A flag flying on the roof
exercise: «The perimeter of a rectangle is twice the length plus twice the width.» What does perimeter mean in this sentence? | The number of corners | The width only | *The length all the way around the rectangle | The area inside the rectangle
test: If the perimeter is 26 cm and the length is 8 cm, what is the width? | *5 cm | 18 cm | 8 cm | 13 cm
test: Dad fences the garden along its perimeter. What does he fence? | The flowerbeds inside | The gate only | The grass in the middle | *The outer edge all the way around
exam: «Careful: the perimeter of such an L is still 2 × (10 + 8) = 36, the cut corner does not change it.» Mia says cutting the corner makes the perimeter smaller. What is true? | The perimeter becomes zero | The perimeter doubles | *Mia is wrong, the perimeter stays 36 | Mia is right, because the edge gets shorter
